    "question": {
      ":question/id": "03ae71cb-e58f-55a0-9871-9f2696aa7962",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{sech}^{2} (\\ln x)dx$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\ln x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nThe limits change from $x=1$ to $u=0$, and from $x=e^{2}$ to $u=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^{e^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\ln x)}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_0^{2}\\operatorname{sech}^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\tanh u\\right]_0^{2} \\\\\n&=\\tanh 2-\\tanh 0 \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{4}-1}{e^{4}+1}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe original interval has $x>0$, so $\\ln x$ and the substitution are defined throughout.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
