      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{e^{4}}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{sech}^{2} (\\ln x)dx$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\ln x$. Differentiating gives $\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nThe limits change from $x=1$ to $u=0$, and from $x=e^{4}$ to $u=4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_1^{e^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}^2(\\ln x)}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_0^{4}\\operatorname{sech}^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\\n&=\\left[\\tanh u\\right]_0^{4} \\\\\n&=\\tanh 4-\\tanh 0 \\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{8}-1}{e^{8}+1}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe original interval has $x>0$, so $\\ln x$ and the substitution are defined throughout.",
